<h2>The Accidental Generalist</h2>

<p>For over a decade, I've been the <strong>'Swiss Army Knife' developer</strong> that startups and businesses call when they need to build, fix, or scale their online platforms.</p>

<p>This is not a position I consciously prepared for. I didn't even come up with the term "Swiss Army knife" myself; it was something one of my former bosses labeled me with. At first, I was a bit surprised, but upon reflection, it’s a good way to describe the <strong>mindset I embody</strong>.</p>

<p>I've never managed to focus on just one programming language because, at my core, I'm driven by the <strong>puzzle, not the tool</strong>. This started as a kid, spending days solving the mysteries of QBasic or Turbo Pascal. As I evolved, that curiosity meant my <strong>skills were always shaped by the problem at hand</strong>: who needed me, and for what kind of task.</p>

<ul>
    <li>At Yopeso, I <strong>revived a legacy frontend</strong>, turning a neglected project into a deployable solution.</li>
    <li>As a freelancer, I <strong>designed the entire server-side architecture</strong> for a startup that, while not a commercial success, won awards for its innovation.</li>
    <li>At Travelminit, I <strong>led the development of critical features</strong>, including a complex data migration system for our acquisition.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Mindset over Meteor</h2>

<p>Maybe it was because I was always looking for that one technology, framework, project where I could finally camp out and say, <em>"I've arrived."</em> It took <strong>10+ years of hands-on experience</strong> and countless hours of rework to realize that this coveted moment of enlightenment never comes. Not because there isn't one that's right for me; maybe they all are a little bit. But because a list of technologies couldn't tell the whole story. It couldn't explain the <em>HOWs</em>, neither the <em>WHYs</em>. It doesn't act as a <strong>filter</strong> for the kind of work where I can truly flourish.</p>

<p>A few years ago, I applied for a MeteorJS job, thinking it was the logical next step on my path. After the interviews, they came back with a surprise: an offer for a front-end developer role. I hesitated, but my curiosity won, and I said yes.</p>

<p>It turned out they didn't hire a 'framework.' They hired a <strong>mindset</strong>. They needed an <strong>open-minded professional</strong> to fix an AngularJS project no one would touch. It took three months of intensive work, but we got it live. It wasn't the sexiest job, but I've never been prouder of one. It taught me a <strong>crucial lesson</strong> and earned me a reputation that led to a substantial raise months later.</p>

<p>That's the future. People look for a <strong>worldview</strong>, not a list of keywords on a resume.</p>

<p>This is the <strong>filter</strong> I'm building my personal site around.</p>

<h2>The Next Chapter</h2>

<p>And that filter isn't just for a website; it's the <strong>foundation for my next chapter</strong>. I'm now focusing on <strong>digital ownership</strong>, <strong>radical simplicity</strong>, and building things that are meant to last, not just to trend.</p>

<p>Some may say my philosophy is old-school; I'd call it <strong>progressively pragmatic</strong> instead. I see modern tools like <strong>AI</strong> not as a magic "generator," but as a <strong>helpful partner</strong> for sharpening ideas. Likewise, my interest in languages like <strong>Elixir and Go</strong> isn't about chasing trends, but great tools to build <strong>resilient, high-performing systems</strong>. I choose the <strong>right tool for the job</strong>, whether old or new.</p>

<p>Moving forward, I'm looking to collaborate with teams who believe the <strong>"WHY"</strong> we build something is the most important question. A clear mission makes the decisions about <em>what</em> and <em>how</em> to build it fall into place—the purpose should dictate the technology, not the other way around.</p>

<p>If you're building a project with a <strong>clear mission</strong> and need a partner to help you navigate that journey, then we might be a great fit.</p>

<p><strong>If that resonates, I'd love to connect.</strong></p>